{"candidates":[{"content":"All five neighborhood candidates depend on a single covering principle: the answer to 'how many pieces' is determined by two interpretive choices — (1) whether cuts are idealized as zero-width lines or physical kerfs, and (2) whether 'pieces' are counted combinatorially (as regions bounded by cut lines) or topologically (as connected components of the remainder). Each candidate is a projection of one combination: SRC_003 (zero-width + topological = 1 piece), SRC_004 (positive-width or fractal + topological = infinite), SRC_002 (general-position + combinatorial = 11), SRC_005 (kerf + topological = count depends on center singularity), SRC_001 (zero-width + topological = 1). The principle is REFUTED IF any candidate cannot be mapped to a unique coordinate in this two-axis space, or if a candidate's mechanism requires an interpretive dimension beyond cut-width and piece-definition.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"cd41a7efca8af63f02845c813468688f2ab83026e52cc0d14decc34e62cb0823"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0342d559aaecb3ca6c47f1e67c3c485b7f4e16ca2880d487fed30f30899fd8d2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"53b171c169b580b3e58e54f828656325ad72246bf75879017c075427864841af"},{"role":"dependence","target":"9d3a3881f549910dfd9eafa9453b4d9b7781f878944e34f03bdb06933181e2bd"},{"role":"dependence","target":"6a4fa58284ab42fd1d4fb550721ff5e0b31bd13766b4d7ade1bf0154d8ccbdd7"}],"typicality":0.85},{"content":"SRC_001 and SRC_003 share a mechanism — the topological observation that removing a 1-dimensional set (zero-width cut lines) from a 2-dimensional connected open set leaves it path-connected — and therefore reduce to the same underlying claim of a single piece. They are REFUTED IF their counterconditions reveal a structural difference rather than merely a different emphasis: SRC_001 frames the manifold-level argument and lists combinatorial and kerf counterconditions, while SRC_003 frames the same argument as a measure-theoretic/topological one with closure and closed-disk counterconditions. If both countercondition sets collapse to the same two rivals (combinatorial counting and physical kerf), the candidates are equivalent; if SRC_003's closure/disk cases produce a genuinely different remainder structure, they do not reduce.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"cd41a7efca8af63f02845c813468688f2ab83026e52cc0d14decc34e62cb0823"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0342d559aaecb3ca6c47f1e67c3c485b7f4e16ca2880d487fed30f30899fd8d2"}],"typicality":0.75},{"content":"SRC_002 and SCR_002 share a mechanism: the transition from concurrency (8 pieces) to general position (11 pieces) is governed by whether 'passing through the exact center' is read as strict concurrence or approximate near-concurrence. SRC_002 inherits the combinatorial formula n(n+1)/2 + 1, and SCR_002 maps the semantic ambiguity of 'exact' to the mathematical bifurcation. This is REFUTED IF the offset interpretation yields a piece count other than 11 for four lines — for example, if paired offsets produce parallelism that reduces the count below 11 — since then a single formula cannot cover the mechanism.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"6a4fa58284ab42fd1d4fb550721ff5e0b31bd13766b4d7ade1bf0154d8ccbdd7"},{"role":"dependence","target":"9d3a3881f549910dfd9eafa9453b4d9b7781f878944e34f03bdb06933181e2bd"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_004 and SRC_005 share a mechanism: positive cut thickness (or pathological curves) changes the topological count by destroying or fragmenting the center region. SRC_004 projects this to infinity via fractal cuts; SRC_005 projects it to a center-singularity that may disconnect the 8 sectors. They abstract a common principle: when cut width is non-zero the center is no longer a shared boundary, and the count is no longer 8. This is REFUTED IF a finite-thickness model with four straight concurrent cuts yields exactly 8 disconnected pieces with no center-removal effect — which would mean thickness alone, without fractal geometry, is insufficient to change the count, breaking the shared mechanism.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"53b171c169b580b3e58e54f828656325ad72246bf75879017c075427864841af"},{"role":"dependence","target":"9d3a3881f549910dfd9eafa9453b4d9b7781f878944e34f03bdb06933181e2bd"}],"typicality":0.65},{"content":"SRC_003 contradicts SRC_002 because they apply the same pizza-cutting scenario to incompatible interpretive frameworks: SRC_003 adopts a topological measure-theoretic lens yielding 1 piece, while SRC_002 adopts a combinatorial general-position lens yielding 11 pieces. The contradiction is REFUTED IF the two frameworks are shown to be compatible under a unified partition theory — for instance, if the topological remainder (1 connected component) and the combinatorial sectors (11 bounded regions) are recognized as different answers to different questions rather than contradictory answers to the same question, in which case the relation becomes abstraction rather than contradiction.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"0342d559aaecb3ca6c47f1e67c3c485b7f4e16ca2880d487fed30f30899fd8d2"},{"role":"dependence","target":"6a4fa58284ab42fd1d4fb550721ff5e0b31bd13766b4d7ade1bf0154d8ccbdd7"}],"typicality":0.5}],"simulation_proposals":[]}